a difference.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.890
You can actually change things in that
company.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:02.914
I was telling the Dean, when we were
talking just a little while

00:11:02.914 --> 00:11:06.809
ago, when I joined MasterCard, we had 9%
of our population was millennials.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:13.900
It's now four and a half years later, we
closed last year 34% from millennials.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.270
I could never have done that at Citi.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:17.530
I just could not.

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:20.275
&gt;&gt; But can you argue millennials and stock
price are correlated?

00:11:20.275 --> 00:11:24.665
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Not on buying, maybe on doing
something about it.

00:11:24.665 --> 00:11:26.780
[LAUGH] Yeah, that's true.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:31.000
Our stock value has quadrupled in these
four years, that is true, right.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.290
But, that I think has to do with the fact

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.240
that we are doing two things well, laying
our real strategy.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.495
It is simple to understand, and we are
executing

00:11:39.495 --> 00:11:41.920
against it and putting our money where our
mouth is.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:43.970
And if you do that well over a period

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.171
of time, stock markets tend to compensate
you well.

00:11:47.171 --> 00:11:50.248
&gt;&gt; So I'm gonna try something a little bit
different, lets take

00:11:50.248 --> 00:11:52.801
humility put it in a box and throw it out
